  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nSurface high pressure remains overhead across central half of \nthe state this afternoon. Quasi-zonal flow continues across the \nnorthern plains as an upper level wave moves across eastern \nMontana producing showers. This wave has a low chance of \ninitiation of strong to severe storms this afternoon in the\nsouth west as there is decent effective shear around 30 to 35 \nkts and MLCAPE around 1000 j/kg in eastern Montana moving into \nwestern North Dakota. There is some modest cap storms will have \nto overcome into order to be strong to severe. Increasing clouds\ncoverage downstream may help to keep the cap from eroding this\nafternoon. The CAMS keep storms isolated to scattered across \nthe southern half of the state with 20 to 40 percent of showers \nand thunderstorms this evening and tonight. A front will \nprogress with wave pushing east of the area by tomorrow morning.\n\nTemperature this afternoon are forecast to warm into the 80s. \nWhile overnight lows are forecast to drop down into the 50s.\n\nMonday, temperatures will continue to warm into the 80s as an\nupper level ridge out west continues to strengthen. There is \nanother low chance of strong to severe storms in the \nsoutheastern half of the state extending into the very southern \nedge of the James River Valley Monday afternoon. The front will \nbe likely east of the county warning area keeping convection \neast of us. The aforementioned front will bring slightly cool \ntemperatures Tuesday afternoon. Another wave could move through \nthe region Tuesday bringing forth low to medium chances of rain \nacross the state. Low chances for precipitation will continue on\nthrough the week tapering off by the weekend. An upper level \nridge is forecast to build over the region which will lead to \nwarmer weather. This ridge could push temperatures back into the\nupper 80s to lower 90s next weekend.",
